<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  H3X SECURITY AUDIT TOOL ENTITLEMENTS
  ====================================
  Signed by build.sh under the QomputeAI Developer ID with the hardened
  runtime.

  WHY THIS FILE WAS REWRITTEN
  ===========================
  The previous version carried, inside an XML comment, the text
  "h3x_grammar" followed by a double hyphen and "compare-runtime". A
  double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment. plutil lints such a
  file as valid, but AMFI (the parser codesign uses for entitlements)
  rejects the whole thing:

      Failed to parse entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error
      near line 9

  build.sh runs under "set -e", so its codesign step failed on every
  invocation and the signing never completed. That is why every h3x
  binary shipped and installed to date is "adhoc, linker-signed" with
  TeamIdentifier not set and no entitlements at all, and therefore why
  h3x_sentinel could not call task_for_pid: it printed the refusal to
  stderr, exited 0, and emitted an empty profile list that every caller
  read as a clean scan.

  Do not write a double hyphen in these comments. Use "=" to underline
  and name flags without their leading hyphens in prose.

  GRANT ONLY, NEVER DENY EXPLICITLY
  =================================
  The hardened runtime denies every capability that is not granted here,
  so the previous list of keys set to false was redundant. Worse, a key
  written as false is indistinguishable from an absent key at runtime
  while looking deliberate in the file. Library validation, JIT, unsigned
  executable memory, dyld environment overrides, executable page
  protection and get-task-allow are all in their safe state precisely
  because they are not mentioned below.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- task_for_pid, required to read another process's memory: the
	     h3x_sentinel modes, and the compare-runtime mode of h3x_grammar.
	     The only capability the toolkit needs beyond the defaults.

	     Note this is not sufficient on its own. It opens unsigned and
	     third-party targets holding get-task-allow. Hardened targets
	     without it, and Apple platform binaries under SIP, stay closed
	     even to root; those need com.apple.system-task-ports, which is
	     Apple-private. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.debugger</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
